(*
TimerNode — Rung 8 gate 1. Proves the REAL on-target timer backend
(src/backend/zephyr/timer_zephyr.c) with NO radio and NO hardware, on native_sim:

  k_timer ISR -> pulse_post_isr -> k_msgq -> pumpEvents -> sys/timer channel
              -> Timers dispatch -> the node's reaction

A 200ms repeating timer must fire 3 times, then the node CANCELS it and prints TIMER OK.
The cancel is load-bearing, not tidiness: this node returns from main:, so it stops
draining — and an uncancelled repeater then fills the 16-deep pulse mailbox and k_oops()es
the node ~3.4s later (17 fires at 200ms). Every other node blocks forever draining, so
this is the only node shape that can leak a live repeater. Note that
`platform pumpEvents` BLOCKS (K_FOREVER) on the pulse mailbox between ticks, so this
node also demonstrates that the VM IDLES the CPU while waiting on a timer rather than
spinning — the property that lets the Thread node sleep 15s and still yield to the
OpenThread thread.

Node shape is PulseNode's (`packageUsing: manifest`, platform arriving as the `main:`
argument) — this node grants no capability, so it needs no `net:` header.
Dialect: `[ ... ] whileTrue:`, never `repeat`. Until rete#244 `platform timers` answered a FRESH Timers
each call (it owns the nsl-side pending registry), so hold ONE instance.
expect:
TIMER OK
*)
class TimerNode packageUsing: manifest = ( | ticks ::= 0. | ) (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space timers ping tok |
    space:: platform space.
    timers:: platform timers.     (* hold ONE instance — it owns the pending registry *)
    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :e | timers dispatch: e ].
    ping:: space channel: 'tick'.
    ping whenever: '' do: [ :m | ticks:: ticks + 1 ].
    tok:: timers every: 200 post: #t on: ping.
    [ ticks < 3 ] whileTrue: [ platform pumpEvents. space drain ].
    timers cancel: tok.   (* MUST cancel before ^: this node RETURNS, so it stops
       draining, but an uncancelled repeater keeps firing into the 16-deep pulse
       mailbox — 17 fires later pulse_post_isr k_oops()es the node. Every other node
       blocks forever draining, so this node is the only one that can leak a live
       repeater. The token IS the right to cancel; holding it is the whole point. *)
    'TIMER OK' println.
    ^ 0
  )
) : (
)
